WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.550
A number that looked boring to a Cambridge
professor turned out to be mathematically

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:04.280
extraordinary.

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:05.560
In 1918,

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:10.230
the mathematician G. H. Hardy visited
an Indian mathematician named Srinivasa

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:11.700
Ramanujan in hospital.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.003
Hardy mentioned that the taxi he arrived
in had a rather boring number: 1729.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.040
But Ramanujan immediately disagreed.

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.580
He said 1729 was actually
a very interesting number.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:26.890
It was the smallest number that could be
expressed as the sum of two positive cubes

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.280
in two different ways.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:32.220
First: 1 cubed plus 12 cubed equals 1729.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.090
Second: 9 cubed plus 10
cubed also equals 1729.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.470
Two completely different combinations.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:39.730
The same number!

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.730
That's why 1729 became famous
as the Hardy–Ramanujan number.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.630
Imagine looking at an ordinary taxi
number and seeing something extraordinary.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:48.850
Would you have noticed it?

